---
title: Add a CPM dependency
description: Register a source dependency and link its exported CMake target to an existing C++ target.
section: How-to guides
order: 4
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

Use this procedure in a Rivet-owned C++ project. You need an existing consumer target and the package's repository, revision, and exported CMake target. C projects use custom CMake; other languages use their native package tools.

## Register the dependency

This example links fmt to an existing target named `engine_core`:

```sh
rivet dependency fmt \
  --repository fmtlib/fmt \
  --tag 11.2.0 \
  --target fmt::fmt \
  --link-to engine_core \
  --preview
```

Review the plan, then repeat without `--preview` to apply it. In Neovim, `:RivetDependency` asks for the same fields and displays the changes.

`--target` is the dependency's exported CMake target; `--link-to` is your consumer. The [fmt build instructions](https://fmt.dev/11.2/get-started/) document `fmt::fmt`, and [release 11.2.0](https://github.com/fmtlib/fmt/releases/tag/11.2.0) provides the tag used here. Check those values against each dependency's own documentation.

`--version` is an alias for `--tag`. Both pass the exact revision to Git; Rivet does not resolve version ranges or add a `v` prefix. A full commit ID gives a stronger pin than a movable tag.

## Configure and verify

```sh
rivet build --preset debug
rivet test --preset debug
```

Applying the plan updates `.rivet.json`, `cmake/Dependencies.cmake`, and generated target linkage. The next Configure fetches packages as needed; Build and Test both configure first.

A configure error saying the expected target was not defined means the package did not export your `--target` value. Check its spelling and build options. Managed-file conflicts are covered in [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting/).

Commit the manifest and generated declarations with the project. Keep custom `find_package` calls and package-specific settings in your own CMake extensions; see [CMake file ownership](/docs/cmake/#know-which-files-to-edit).

## Download behavior

The dependency-free general C++ starter can configure offline when its tools are installed. Once a dependency is registered, Configure loads the CPM bootstrap and downloads sources when absent from cache.

Rivet pins CPM to **0.43.1**, verifies its embedded SHA256 before inclusion, and checks cached copies too. The bootstrap and dependency source cache live under the ignored `.cache/CPM` directory. This verifies CPM itself; transitive package revisions still depend on the packages' own declarations.
